---
# The one door that lets monitor-bridge (daniel-server) query this cluster's Prometheus.
#
# Slice 3, D8. Prometheus' node-side listener is pinned to 127.0.0.1 by claude_otel_query_host_ip
# — deliberately, so `otelq` can reach it while the LAN cannot — and its Service is ClusterIP.
# Both are correct and neither is changed here: monitor-bridge runs on daniel-server, so it can
# reach neither, and the k3s API server's own :6443 is firewalled off from daniel-server too
# (measured 2026-08-07: connect fails outright). This route is the deliberate exception.
#
# Modelled on the n8n-monitoring route (roles/k8s/n8n/templates/ingressroute.yaml.j2), which
# exists for exactly this problem and is the pattern to copy rather than invent from: LAN-only
# host, matched paths only, rate-limited.
#
# NARROW ON PURPOSE — three separate reasons this is a query-only door:
#   1. PathPrefix `/api/v1/query` is the ONLY path check.py fetches (check.py:644 and :658 both
#      call _instant_query(PROM_URL, "/api/v1/query", ...)). It also admits /api/v1/query_range,
#      which is a prefix match and is likewise read-only.
#   2. Prometheus' admin API (/api/v1/admin/tsdb/delete_series) requires --web.enable-admin-api
#      and the lifecycle endpoints (/-/reload, /-/quit) require --web.enable-lifecycle. Neither
#      flag is set on the Deployment, so those endpoints do not exist to be reached.
#   3. No `/graph`, `/targets` or `/metrics`. Browsing the TSDB is Grafana's job and it already
#      has an authenticated route.
#
# LAN-ONLY, and unlike the shared ingressroute() macro this does NOT honour k8s_public_route.
# That is intentional: when slice 6 flips that flag for the app routes, this one must not follow
# them onto the public internet. There is no authentication in front of it. Since 2026-08-13
# the read rule also carries ClientIP({{ lan_subnet }}) — deliberately the whole LAN, not a
# host pin (operator decision: any LAN computer may query), but pods (10.42/16) and WireGuard
# peers (10.8/24) present their real source IPs here and are excluded, closing the two
# non-LAN populations that could otherwise read infra topology and Claude Code usage metrics.
apiVersion: traefik.io/v1alpha1
kind: IngressRoute
metadata:
  name: prometheus-monitoring
  namespace: {{ k8s_observability_namespace }}
spec:
  entryPoints:
    - https
  routes:
    - kind: Rule
      # LAN-wide by operator decision (2026-08-13): any LAN computer may query; pods and
      # WireGuard peers are excluded. The two /31s are the NODE-owned flannel.1/cni0
      # addresses (10.42.0.0-1 = daniel-box, 10.42.1.0-1 = daniel-server): a host-originated
      # query to the VIP takes kube-proxy's cluster-policy path and SNATs to one of them
      # (measured via authelia's remote_ip: daniel-box curls arrive as 10.42.0.1,
      # daniel-server's as 10.42.1.0 — host-originated traffic never traverses the
      # vip-kube-bypass, which is PREROUTING-only). Pods are always .2+ and stay excluded.
      # Container traffic from daniel-server (monitor-bridge) DOES ride the bypass and
      # arrives LAN-masqueraded as 10.0.0.161. OR'd matchers, NOT ClientIP(a, b): the
      # multi-arg form fails rule parsing on this Traefik and the router vanishes.
      match: "Host(`prometheus{{ k8s_hostname_suffix }}.local.{{ domain }}`) && PathPrefix(`/api/v1/query`) && (ClientIP(`{{ lan_subnet }}`) || ClientIP(`10.42.0.0/31`) || ClientIP(`10.42.1.0/31`))"
      middlewares:
        - name: rate-limit
      services:
        - name: prometheus
          port: 9090
    # The northbound write door (slice 3, B3). A SEPARATE route rather than a widened match, so
    # the read door above stays as narrow as it was: the two have disjoint paths and disjoint
    # guards, and neither inherits the other's. Folding write into the query rule would have made
    # every LAN host able to inject metrics.
    #
    # ClientIP is the whole control here — there is no authentication in front of Prometheus, and
    # remote-write is an unauthenticated append to the TSDB that monitor-bridge will alert from
    # after B5. Two facts make the guard real rather than decorative, both checked 2026-08-07
    # rather than assumed:
    #   1. Traefik matches ClientIP against the TCP peer address, NOT X-Forwarded-For, so a
    #      LAN host cannot claim to be daniel-server by setting a header.
    #   2. The cluster Traefik Service is externalTrafficPolicy=Local
    #      (`kubectl get svc traefik -n homelab -o jsonpath='{.spec.externalTrafficPolicy}'`).
    #      Under `Cluster` kube-proxy would SNAT the source to a node IP and this matcher would
    #      match nothing at all — so that setting is load-bearing for this route, not incidental.
    # Same /32 as the strangler bridge's own guard (k8s_bridge_client_ip, group_vars/all.yml).
    #
    # No rate-limit middleware, unlike the read route, and that asymmetry is on purpose. When the
    # sender has been unable to write for a while it shards up and replays its backlog as fast as
    # the receiver accepts it — precisely the moment it would blow past 300 req/min and start
    # taking 429s. Remote-write treats those as failures and eventually drops the samples, so a
    # limiter here would convert a brief outage into permanent gaps. The single-source ClientIP
    # guard already bounds who can generate this load.
    - kind: Rule
      match: "Host(`prometheus{{ k8s_hostname_suffix }}.local.{{ domain }}`) && PathPrefix(`/api/v1/write`) && ClientIP(`{{ k8s_bridge_client_ip }}/32`)"
      services:
        - name: prometheus
          port: 9090
  tls:
    certResolver: cloudflare
    options:
      name: modern
    domains:
      - main: "{{ domain }}"
        sans:
          - "*.{{ domain }}"
          - "*.local.{{ domain }}"
